+ * A Z24S8 depth target read as a linear A8R8G8B8 texture: a deferred renderer
+ * (GT5) rebuilding depth from the bytes. Without this the engine stores the
+ * depth into guest memory for it -- a wait for the GPU, a full readback, a
+ * CPU conversion and the texture decoded and uploaded again, twice a frame
+ * for GT5's 2048x1080 depth. Here it stays on the GPU, in command order:
+ * depth -> a storage buffer (the copy gives a 32-bit word per texel: a float
+ * for D32, the 24-bit value in the low bits for D24), a compute pass packs
+ * each word in place into the texel the decode would give, buffer -> an
+ * RGBA8 image. R = depth bits 15..8, G = bits 7..0, B = the stencil byte
+ * (copied out beside the depth words) -- GT5 draws its text by inverting a
+ * stencil bit per glyph and reads the coverage back from B -- and A = bits
+ * 23..16,
+ * with round(d * 0xFFFFFF) taken exactly in 32-bit integers (no Int64 on most
+ * phones): d = m * 2^-s, so the value is (m * (2^24 - 1) + 2^(s-1)) >> s.
+ * DEPTH16 of a Z16 zeta, and D16 devices, keep the guest-memory store.
